    "8": [
      "The eighth chapter, “Immunity, Stress, and Disease”, introduces psychoneuroimmunology, the study of the remarkable connections between our thoughts, brain and immune defences.",
      "The chapter challenges popular myths about stress and illness, explaining how we become vulnerable to infections and why “positive thinking” does not cure cancer. These are its main concepts:",
      "1. Immune swings: a surprise for scientists",
      "For a long time, stress was thought simply to suppress immunity to save energy. More recent discoveries, however, revealed a much more complex response:",
      "* Activation phase: During the first 30 minutes of stress, immunity does not decline but increases sharply. Stress hormones, including glucocorticoids and adrenaline, do not deactivate immune cells; they urgently move them from the blood to the frontline, such as the skin, so wounds can heal faster after injury.",
      "* Suppression phase: If stress persists, glucocorticoids begin selectively destroying lymphocytes—immune cells—starting with the oldest and least useful.",
      "* Why suppress immunity? Scientist Allan Munck found that the body deliberately brakes the immune system after its initial mobilisation to prevent an excessive response. If hyperactive immunity is not switched off, it may confuse the body’s own tissues with foreign ones, leading to autoimmune diseases such as multiple sclerosis or rheumatoid arthritis. Failure of this braking system increases autoimmune risk.",
      "2. Pathways to illness",
      "Why do we get ill more often under stress? Sapolsky distinguishes two pathways:",
      "* The psychoneuroimmune pathway: Stress releases hormones that physiologically suppress immunity and increase the likelihood of infection.",
      "* The lifestyle pathway: Under stress or depression, people sleep and eat poorly, forget medication or fail to wear a warm coat in freezing weather. Often it is the stress-related change in lifestyle, rather than the hormones themselves, that causes illness.",
      "3. Colds, herpes and the ingenuity of viruses",
      "* Common colds: Epidemiological evidence and experiments confirm that chronic stress increases the risk of catching a cold.",
      "* Latent viruses: Herpes viruses hide in nerve cells for years, waiting for an opportunity to multiply. How do they detect weakened immunity? Their DNA contains a special sensor that responds to increased blood glucocorticoids. Herpes can also perform a remarkable trick: it stimulates the hypothalamus to produce stress hormones, creating favourable conditions for a flare-up.",
      "4. Cancer and the mythology of miracle cures",
      "Perhaps the most challenging part concerns stress and cancer. In animals, severe stress can accelerate the growth of existing tumours because glucocorticoids promote blood-vessel growth into them—angiogenesis—and supply glucose. In humans, however, there is no reliable evidence that stress causes cancer.",
      "Sapolsky sharply criticises physicians such as Bernie Siegel, author of Love, Medicine and Miracles, who claim that cancer results from insufficient spirituality and can be cured by love and courage alone. This approach is cruel because it blames the victim for incurable illness: if the patient dies, they supposedly did not want to live enough or did not love enough. Cancer is an enormously complex biological problem, not punishment for an incorrect psychological attitude.",
      "5. A historical tragedy: status thymicolymphaticus",
      "The chapter ends with the tragic story of a medical disaster caused by overlooking stress. In the nineteenth century, doctors autopsying infants from poor families—whose chronic stress had shrunk their thymus glands—assumed that a tiny thymus was normal. When a child from a wealthy family died of sudden infant death syndrome, a normal, larger thymus led doctors to diagnose the imaginary disease status thymicolymphaticus. Believing that a healthy thymus compressed the windpipe, they widely prescribed neck irradiation for infants. This caused tens of thousands of thyroid cancers in the twentieth century, stemming from a failure to understand how poverty and stress affect anatomy.",
      "Chapter conclusion: Our immunity is finely tuned to brief stressful crises but vulnerable to chronic anxiety. At the same time, we should avoid extremes and not attribute every human illness exclusively to psychological stress."
    ],
